2 TITL Champion English COA
1 _OID c436e533-65d4-4ae9-93ba-67333b5c37f8
1 PLAC From www.HouseOfNames.com Archives copyright © 2000 - 2009
1 _DSCR Origin Displayed: English - Origins Available: Belgium, English-Alt, English. Spelling variations of this family name include: Champion, Campion and others. First found in Bedfordshire , where they were seated from very early times and were grante
2 CONC d lands by Duke William of Normandy, their liege Lord, for their distinguished assistance at the Battle of Hastings in 1066 C.E. Some of the first settlers of this family name or some of its variants were: John Champion who settled in Virginia in 1
2 CONC 635; Richard Champion settled in 1634; Thomas Champion arrived in New England in 1634 with servants; Zachariah Champion settled in Charleston S. (From www.HouseOfNames.com Archives copyright © 2000 - 2009) This most interesting surname is of Anglo-
2 CONC French origin, and is an occupational name for a professional champion, especially an agent employed to represent one of the parties in a trial by combat, a method of settling disputes in the Middle Ages. The name itself comes from the Old French "
2 CONC champiun", Old Norman French "campiun", and Anglo-Norman French "campion", champion. In this ordeal by battle, in criminal cases, the accuser and the accused took the field themselves, but in disputes over ownership of land, the actual parties wer
2 CONC e represented by "champions"; in theory, their free tenants, but in practice, hired men, professional champions who were very well paid. In 1294, the Dean and Chapter of Southwell paid 750, in modern money, in hiring a champion to dual in settlin
2 CONC g a law-suit about the advowson of a church. A "pugil" or champion was a regular member of the household of more than one medieval bishop; Thomas Cantilupe, Bishop of Hereford (1275 - 1282), paid his champion, Thomas de Bruges, a salary of half a m
2 CONC ark a year. In some instances the name may be of Old French locational origin, from Compiegne in Oise, Picardy. Herbert Campion was recorded in 1148, in the Book of Winton (Hampshire), and Pascoe Champion, aged 23 yrs., was one of the muster of Cap
2 CONC tain William Tucker in Virginia, having sailed there on the "Ellonor" in 1621. The first recorded spelling of the family name is shown to be that of Nicole de Campion, which was dated circa 1096, accompanied Robert 2nd, Duke of Normandy, on the fir
2 CONC st crusade (1096 - 1099), in "Crusade Records", during the reign of King William 2nd, known as "Rufus", 1087 - 1100. Surnames became necessary when governments introduced personal taxation. In England this was known as Poll Tax. Throughout the cent
2 CONC uries, surnames in every country have continued to "develop" often leading to astonishing variants of the original spelling. © Copyright: Name Origin Research www.surnamedb.com 1980 - 2014.
