2 TITL James Monroe Military
1 _OID d0104087-c15a-43bf-a8f7-5f8378151d1b
1 DATE 1776 to 1858
1 _DSCR Copied from the SAR of 1959
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;While a student at the College of William and Mary, James Monroe joined the Continental army. &amp;nbsp;He served as a Lieutenant in the Third Virginia Regiment under Colnel Hugh Mercer. &amp;nbsp;He took part i
2 CONC n the Battles of Harlem Heights, White Plains, Brandywine, Germantown, Monmouth, Trenton and spend the winter at Valley Forge with General Washington. &amp;nbsp;At Trenton he was wounded by a British musketball in the shoulder, which he bore with h
2 CONC im to the grave. &amp;nbsp;During the latter part of the War, he served as Military Commissioner for Governor Thomas Jefferson to the southern theatre of war. &amp;nbsp;He obtained the rank of Lieutenant Colonel.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Gene
2 CONC ral Washington, in a letter to Governor Archibald Cary of Virginia, summarizes the part taken by James Monroe in the Revolution: &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;ldquo;The zeal he discovered by entering the service at an early period
2 CONC , the character he supported in his regiment, and the manner in which he received a wound, induced me to appoint him to a captaincy in one of the additional regiments. &amp;nbsp;This regiment failing, from the difficulty of recruiting, he entered i
2 CONC n (General) Lord Stirling&amp;rsquo;s Family and has served two campaigns as a volunteer aid to his lordship. &amp;nbsp;He has in every instance maintained the reputation of a brave, active, and sensible officer,&amp;rdquo;. It is interesting to no
2 CONC te that AJames monroe - soldier, lawyer, diplomat, statesman, President of the United States, died on the Fourth of July 1851 in New York City and was buried in Marble Cemetery on lower Second Street, his remains were removed to Hollywood Cemetery
2 CONC , Richmond, Virginia, and were re-interred on AJuly 5 1858, the year of the centennial of his birth in Westmoreland County, Virginia.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
